A normal random variable has mean $45$ and standard deviation $6$.
Where are the points of inflection of its curve?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, x=1.1cm, y=1cm]
\draw[thick] plot[domain=-3.6:3.6, samples=90, smooth, variable=\x] ({\x},{2.4*exp(-\x*\x/2)});
\draw[->] (-4,0) -- (4.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw (0,0) -- (0,-0.12) node[below, font=\small] {$45$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A normal curve is symmetrical about $x=24$, and one of its points of inflection is at $x=30$.
What is the standard deviation?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, x=1.1cm, y=1cm]
\draw[thick] plot[domain=-3.6:3.6, samples=90, smooth, variable=\x] ({\x},{2.4*exp(-\x*\x/2)});
\draw[->] (-4,0) -- (4.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\fill (1,1.46) circle (2pt);
\draw[dashed] (1,0) -- (1,1.46);
\draw (0,0) -- (0,-0.12) node[below, font=\small] {$24$};
\draw (1,0) -- (1,-0.12) node[below, font=\small] {$30$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$X$ is normal with mean $30$ and standard deviation $4$.
$Y$ is normal with mean $42$ and standard deviation $4$.
How does the curve of $Y$ compare with the curve of $X$?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, x=1.1cm, y=1cm]
\draw[thick] plot[domain=-3.6:3.6, samples=90, smooth, variable=\x] ({\x},{2.4*exp(-\x*\x/2)});
\draw[->] (-4,0) -- (4.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw (0,0) -- (0,-0.12) node[below, font=\small] {$30$};
\node[right, font=\small] at (1.2,1.9) {curve of $X$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A normal curve has mean $82$.
What is the area under the curve to the left of $x=82$?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, x=1.1cm, y=1cm]
\fill[gray!35] (-3.6,0) -- plot[domain=-3.6:0, samples=60, smooth, variable=\x] ({\x},{2.4*exp(-\x*\x/2)}) -- (0,0) -- cycle;
\draw[thick] plot[domain=-3.6:3.6, samples=90, smooth, variable=\x] ({\x},{2.4*exp(-\x*\x/2)});
\draw[->] (-4,0) -- (4.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw (0,0) -- (0,-0.12) node[below, font=\small] {$82$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

